## Summary / key design implications for the new feature

1. **Reuse, don't reinvent:** The maintenance-strategy interface/enum/codeunit infrastructure (Core: `MaintenanceStrategy.Interface.al`, `.Enum.al`, `DefaultMaintenance.Codeunit.al`; Fleet: `HeavyDutyMaintenance.Codeunit.al`, `FleetStrategies.EnumExt.al`) and `FleetMgt.NextServiceKm` already compute the "next service km" threshold (15,000/5,000 km defaults). No new interface/enum is needed — just a comparison against `Vehicle.Mileage`.
2. **Best hook point:** `CGR Fleet Mgt.IsAvailable` — either extend its fallback expression (`not Vehicle."Checked Out" and not Vehicle.Blocked and not <due for service>`) or add a new `[EventSubscriber]` on `OnBeforeIsAvailable` (following the `CGR Rental Fleet Subscribers.SuspendRentals` pattern) that sets `Result := false; IsHandled := true` when `Vehicle.Mileage >= FleetMgt.NextServiceKm(VehicleNo)`.
3. **Gap to fix/flag:** `CGR Rental Mgt.SwapVehicle` does not call `FleetMgt.IsAvailable` — it duplicates checks inline. Any new due-for-service rule added only to `IsAvailable`/`OnBeforeIsAvailable` will be silently skipped during vehicle swaps unless `SwapVehicle` is updated to call `FleetMgt.IsAvailable(NewVehicleNo)` or the same check is duplicated there.
